.TH YUMED 8 "2026-08-31" "YUME 0.3.0-dev1" "YUME Manual"
.SH NAME
yumed \- YUME secure-session web-front-door daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yumed
.BI "--config " path
.RI [ --validate ]
.RI [ --diagnostic-level " level" ]
.br
.B yumed
.B --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yumed
is the intended server runtime for YUME Transport Protocol 1. It will own the
genuine HTTP/2 website or reverse-proxy front door, cheap replay-protected
admission, TLS 1.3 promotion, authenticated named services, and explicit direct
TCP/UDP routing.
.PP
The current 0.3.0-dev1 tree does not yet implement that production runtime.
Setup and doctor can generate and validate a server kit, but no current command
starts a qualified public YTP/1 listener. See
.B docs/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md
before deployment.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--config " path
Select one strict server schema-1 configuration. Listen addresses, cover,
credentials, services, routes, and limits have no CLI override.
.TP
.B --validate
Validate configuration and credentials without listening.
.TP
.BI "--diagnostic-level " level
Select bounded local diagnostic verbosity. Never log key, PSK, exporter, AUTH,
or application plaintext values.
.TP
.B --version
Print the complete compatibility manifest.
.SH PUBLIC INGRESS CONTRACT
Invalid, missing, expired, replayed, or mismatched admission remains ordinary
cover traffic and must not produce a YUME-specific public response. Promotion
to the session engine occurs only after the exact TLS, HTTP/2, admission, and
provider contracts pass. There is no fallback secure-channel backend or YTP
suite downgrade.
.SH PRIVILEGE
Run as a dedicated unprivileged user. Prefer a high port or grant only the
specific low-port binding capability needed for 443. The key-holding process
does not need a shell, firewall administration, package management, or
arbitrary filesystem access.
.SH OUTSIDE YTP/1
Federation, transit, directory, relay applications, reverse administration,
the reserved, disabled transport-v2 EXEC relay-policy surface,
host-controller modes, product codecs, security modes, helper TLS backends, and
the command/control facade are not part of YTP/1. Runnable transport-v2
surfaces and explicitly disabled policy boundaries remain a separate lane.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR yume (1)
